Here's the starting CSS:

```css
.row        { display: flex; width: 600px; height: 200px; }
.row > div  { width: 80px; height: 60px; }
```

Then one more declaration was added to `.row`.

Here's the starting CSS:

```css
.stack       { display: flex; flex-direction: column; width: 600px; height: 300px; }
.stack > div { width: 80px; height: 50px; }
```

Then one more declaration was added to `.stack`.

Three 80px-wide boxes in a 600px-wide flex row:

```css
.row        { display: flex; width: 600px; }
.row > div  { width: 80px; height: 60px; }
```

Five 160 × 60 items wrap onto two flex lines:

```css
.tags        { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; width: 600px; height: 300px; }
.tags > span { width: 160px; height: 60px; }
```

Three cards that grow to fill the row:

```css
.cards        { display: flex; width: 660px; }
.cards > div  { flex: 1; height: 60px; }
```

All three cards are the same width. The outer cards touch the container edges, and there are 30px between neighboring cards.

The items start with different widths:

```css
.row          { display: flex; width: 600px; }
.row > :nth-child(1) { width: 40px; }
.row > :nth-child(2) { width: 120px; }
.row > :nth-child(3) { width: 200px; }
```

Then one declaration was added to all three items.

Same starting CSS as before:

```css
.row          { display: flex; width: 600px; }
.row > :nth-child(1) { width: 40px; }
.row > :nth-child(2) { width: 120px; }
.row > :nth-child(3) { width: 200px; }
```

This time the rendered widths are 120, 200, and 280px.

```css
.row { display: flex; width: 500px; }
.a   { width: 100px; flex: 1 1 auto; }
.b   { width: 100px; flex: 2 1 auto; }
```

Enter a number.

```css
.board {
  display: grid;
  width: 600px;
  height: 300px;
  grid-template-columns: 100px 100px;
  grid-template-rows: 60px 60px;
}
```

Each of the four items contains a single digit.

```css
.grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
  grid-auto-rows: 70px;
  gap: 10px;
}
.item-1 { grid-column: span 3; }
.item-2 { grid-column: span 2; }
```

The items are in DOM order 1, 2, 3, 4, yet item 3 renders *above* item 2.

```css
.grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
  grid-auto-rows: 70px;
  gap: 10px;
}
.item-1, .item-2, .item-5 { grid-column: span 2; }
```

The picture shows the default placement. Answer with the item's number.

